 "capsule": "Despite the label, Monaco was never actually elevated to a kingdom in this era: the 1815 Congress of Vienna and 1817 Treaty of Stupinigi placed the Grimaldi-ruled Principality of Monaco under the protection of the Kingdom of Sardinia, whose own king held that royal title, and Italian, not French, served as Monaco's official language throughout the protectorate. Internal unrest culminated in 1848 when the towns of Menton and Roquebrune declared themselves free, hoping for Sardinian annexation; Sardinia declined, and the reigning prince ultimately ceded the towns to France in 1861 for four million francs. Sardinian protection ended with the 1860 Treaty of Turin, after which Monaco reverted to French protection under the 1861 Franco-Monegasque Treaty, a relationship that persisted through the interwar years until the eve of Monaco's World War II-era occupation.",
